The temperature rise generated during metal cutting governs the magnitude and stability of plastic deformation, and therefore directly influences chip formation, surface integrity, and tool performance [ 10 ]. Although higher temperatures facilitate dislocation movement and expand the elastoplastic zone [ 11 ], under high-speed cutting, they may destabilize plastic flow, alter chip morphology, and deteriorate surface quality [ 12 ]. A substantial fraction of the cutting energy is converted into heat through plastic deformation and friction, which accelerates local deformation rates and directly affects tool life [ 13 ,  14 – 15 ]. Elevated temperatures can further undermine thermo-mechanical stability by promoting energy accumulation within the primary shear zone and inducing microstructural changes [ 16 ,  17 ], implying that both material response and machining parameters control deformation stability [ 11 ,  18 ]. Using numerical and experimental approaches, several studies have investigated chip formation mechanisms in AA7075 under high-speed machining conditions. For instance, Jomaa et al. [ 1 ] reported that increasing cutting speed ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
